+## Provider ABI modes
+
+The module defaults to the historical provider ABI:
+
+```bash
+insmod openurma_ubcore.ko udma_compat=0   # default, driver_name=openurma
+insmod openurma_ubcore.ko udma_compat=1   # stock liburma_udma.so, driver_name=udma
+```
+
+`legacy` retains the original whole-aperture mmap and kernel-side 64-byte flit
+path.  `udma` implements the official UDMA private UAPI from
+`<uapi/ub/urma/udma/udma_abi.h>`: the context response advertises 64-byte CQEs,
+record-mode queues, no direct WQE, and at least one receive SGE; token, JFC,
+JFR, and duplex-Jetty private data are copied and validated at their exact ABI
+sizes.  Phase 1 intentionally does not expose simplex JFS.  Direct WQE remains
+disabled (`dwqe_enable=0`), while ordinary SQ WQEs may carry up to 256 bytes of
+inline payload.
+
+The `max_jfs_inline_size=256` capability therefore accepts the common
+`urma_perftest -I 128` setting without claiming direct-WQE support.  This is a
+format/capability bound, not a calibrated timing constant.
+
+## UDMA DMA-memory lifetime
+
+Before publishing an ADD record to the NIC, UDMA mode faults and pins every
+userspace range that the simulated device can DMA-read or DMA-write.  Queue
+storage uses `FOLL_WRITE | FOLL_LONGTERM`; an MR adds `FOLL_WRITE` only when its
+registered access includes device write, matching the official driver's rule:
+
+| Resource | Pinned userspace storage |
+|---|---|
+| MR | the complete `[va, va + len)` payload interval |
+| JFC | CQ buffer and the page containing its record-CI/arm doorbell |
+| JFR | RQ buffer, index queue, and the pages containing record-PI and sleep records |
+| duplex Jetty | SQ buffer |
+
+The provider's `jetty_addr` fields remain opaque software handles and are not
+DMA buffers.  Address addition, page rounding, page-array sizing, and partial
+pin results are checked; a create failure rolls back every pin acquired by
+that resource.  Destroy first publishes DELETE so the NIC drops the dynamic
+resource, then marks the writable pages dirty, unpins them, and frees the page
+arrays.  This kernel lifetime is independent of the stock provider's UMMU
+grant bookkeeping and keeps the official provider unmodified.
+
+## UDMA mmap and aperture allocation
+
+The 64-KiB aperture has a fixed *layout*, but queue placement and IDs are
+dynamic:
+
+| Byte range | Use |
+|---|---|
+| `0x0000..0x0fff` | reserved legacy page |
+| `0x1000..0xefff` | dynamically allocated 4-KiB context/queue pages |
+| `0xf000..0xffff` | kernel-to-NIC control mailbox |
+
+The official provider maps its JFC MMIO page immediately after context
+creation, before a JFC exists.  Therefore `(cmd=UDMA_MMAP_JFC_PAGE,index=0)`
+maps the calling context's exclusive page.  A JFC arm doorbell is at `+0x40`.
+`(cmd=UDMA_MMAP_JETTY_DSQE,index=jetty_id)` performs an ownership-checked
+resource-table lookup and maps that Jetty's allocated page; its ordinary
+32-bit producer-index doorbell is at `+0x80`.  The resource ID is not converted
+to a physical offset arithmetically.  Queue destroy revokes the NIC resource,
+but its aperture page is retained until the owning context closes; this avoids
+recycling a physical doorbell page while the official uburma mmap layer still
+tracks a userspace VMA.  Phase 1 requires a 4-KiB guest `PAGE_SIZE`, matching
+the current ARM64 guest and the UDMA hardware page.
+
+## Version-2 NIC control message
+
+`openurma_udma_ctrl.h` is the authoritative wire definition.  Each update is
+one complete, little-endian, 64-byte write transaction assembled at aperture
+offset `0xf000`.  The common header is:
+
+| Offset | Width | Field |
+|---:|---:|---|
+| `0x00` | 4 | magic `0x4d43554f` (bytes `OUCM`) |
+| `0x04` | 2 | version `2` |
+| `0x06` | 1 | opcode: RESET=0, ADD=1, DELETE=2, BIND=3, UNBIND=4 |
+| `0x07` | 1 | resource: DEVICE=0, CONTEXT=1, TOKEN=2, MR=3, JFC=4, JFR=5, JETTY=6 |
+| `0x08` | 4 | monotonically increasing sequence |
+| `0x0c` | 4 | resource-specific flags |
+| `0x10` | 4 | stable resource ID |
+| `0x14` | 4 | owning context ID |
+| `0x18..0x37` | 4 × 8 | `data0..data3` |
+| `0x38..0x3f` | 2 × 4 | `data4..data5` |
+
+ADD payloads have these exact version-2 meanings:
+
+| Resource | flags | data0 | data1 | data2 | data3 | data4 | data5 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| CONTEXT | bit 31 PGD-PFN-valid, bits 0..7 PGD PFN[39:32] | EID bytes 0..7 | EID bytes 8..15 | JFC-page aperture offset | hardware page bytes | EID index | PGD PFN[31:0] |
+| TOKEN | generic token flags | exact provider token ID | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| MR | generic register-segment flags | user VA | length | IOVA | token ID | EID index | 0 |
+| JFC | bit 29 hugepage, low bits mode | CQ VA | record-CI VA | context JFC-page offset | CQ byte length | requested depth | CEQ number |
+| JFR | bits 0..19 receive-JFC ID, 20..23 `ceil_log2(roundup_pow2(max_sge))`, 24..26 transport mode, 28/29 non-pin/hugepage | RQ VA | index-queue VA | record-PI VA | provider RQ handle | allocated RQ bytes | actual WQE depth |
+| JETTY | bits 16..19 type, 20..23 transport mode, 27..29 PI/non-pin/hugepage | SQ VA | provider SQ handle | `recv_jfc_id<<32 \| DSQE-page aperture offset` | `jfr_id<<32 \| send_jfc_id` | SQ bytes | SQE BB count |
+
+DELETE uses the same resource/ID/context key (and retains a page offset in
+`data2` for CONTEXT/JETTY; Jetty DELETE carries only its page offset in the low
+32 bits) so the NIC removes the exact dynamic entry; the page becomes eligible
+for recycling when its context closes.  JETTY BIND carries remote Jetty ID in `data0`, the
+remote EID in `data1/data2`, and target type, TP type, and transport mode in
+`data3`; UNBIND clears that association.  RESET brackets module lifetime.
+There are no fixed user virtual addresses or assumed queue IDs in this
+protocol.
+
+For JFR, the stock provider's exact RQE stride is
+`roundup_pow2(max_sge) * 16`; phase 1 advertises `jfr_sge=1` and
+`max_jfr_sge=1`, so the normal value is 16 bytes.  `data5` is the exact provider
+WQE count (`max(64, roundup_pow2(requested_depth))`).  It is carried explicitly:
+both the RQ allocation and index allocation are rounded up to 4 KiB, so neither
+allocation byte length can recover the logical depth for a small queue.
+
+The stock provider also reaches the newer ubcore control-plane contract even
+when the application calls the legacy high-level import API.  This module
+therefore supplies `get_tp_list`, `active_tp`/`deactive_tp`,
+`import_jetty_ex`, and `bind_jetty_ex`.  It returns one non-zero, dynamically
+allocated TP handle carrying the requested CTP/RTP/UTP flags; the simulator
+does not maintain a second hardware TP table.  UDMA-mode JFR and Jetty IDs are
+limited to `0..65535`, matching the provider's fixed 32-by-2048 lookup tables.
+
+For CONTEXT, the kernel captures `virt_to_phys(current->mm->pgd)` while
+`alloc_ucontext` runs in the creating task.  The 40-bit page-frame number spans
+`flags[7:0]` and `data5`, covering arm64's 52-bit physical-address limit while
+preserving every version-1 payload field.  The simulator requires version 2,
+the valid bit, a 4-KiB granule, a page-aligned non-zero root, and a complete
+64-byte message whose root lies in modeled memory.  It never guesses the
+address space from CPU 0, so a context created by a benchmark pinned to CPU 2
+still walks that benchmark process's mappings.
+
+The legacy mode retains the original kernel-side flit path.  In
+`udma_compat=1`, the kernel/private-ABI layer only publishes resources and
+maintains their memory lifetime; there is deliberately no kernel-side data-path
+fallback.  The gem5 NIC consumes the version-2 messages above, fetches the
+official provider's SQ/RQ entries, moves payload bytes between guest memories,
+and writes official 64-byte CQEs.  This split keeps the official UDMA userspace
+provider unmodified and makes the simulated hardware boundary explicit.
+
+The current phase covers normal pinned memory.  It rejects queue/MR requests
+that select UDMA `non_pin` or the driver's dedicated hugepage pool rather than
+silently giving them normal-memory semantics.  Those modes, asynchronous
+resource-destroy drain/ack, simplex JFS, and an independent UMMU translation
+table remain hardware-contract work for the next phase; they must not be
+inferred from the normal-memory success below.
+
